WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.960
A story that may have been too dark&nbsp;
for Disney, controversial songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.120
and in-joke easter eggs are what you’re&nbsp;
about to learn in the next 13 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.160
This is the untold truth of&nbsp;
The Hunchback of Notre Dame.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.720
In 1993, Disney dream team Gary Trousdale&nbsp;
and Kirk Wise got a phone call from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.640
then-chairman of Walt Disney Studios&nbsp;
Jeffrey Katzenberg, who informed them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.440
they would be directing an adaptation of&nbsp;
Victor Hugo's "The Hunchback of Notre Dame."

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.080
Wise and Trousdale had a short but colorful&nbsp;
history with the company. They collaborated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.640
directly for the first time on "The Rescuers&nbsp;
Down Under" — from which they were both fired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.200
But they reunited for their directorial debut,&nbsp;
"Beauty and the Beast," which proved to be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.400
enormous success. It became the first animated&nbsp;
feature to be nominated for the Academy Award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.440
for Best Picture, and won Oscars for Best&nbsp;
Original Score and Best Original Song.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.480
Wise and Trousdale took a break from&nbsp;
directing after the grueling "Beauty" process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.840
But once Katzenberg ordained&nbsp;
"Hunchback" as their follow-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.000
they became determined to push the&nbsp;
boundaries of animation like never before.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.960
Despite its reputation for delivering&nbsp;
happily-ever-afters, Disney frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.360
draws on grim source material. Even so, Victor&nbsp;
Hugo's 1831 novel, "The Hunchback of Notre Dame,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.880
might be the least obvious choice for cartoon&nbsp;
adaptation they could have possibly gone with.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.920
Hugo's tome is a chronicle of human tragedy -&nbsp;
and it's one that has had more faithful film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.600
adaptations in the past. In the original&nbsp;
story, Archdeacon Frollo and Quasimodo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.640
both fall in love with Esmerelda, as they do&nbsp;
in the Disney version. But in the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.560
Frollo orders Quasimodo to kidnap her. This&nbsp;
plan is thwarted, and Quasimodo is arrested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.400
In time, Esmeralda falls in love with Phoebus,&nbsp;
who is considerably less charming than he is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.600
Disney's adaptation. Frollo makes an attempt&nbsp;
on his life, for which Esmerelda is blamed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.120
After Esmeralda refuses his advances, Frollo&nbsp;
hands her over to the authorities. Powerless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.800
Quasimodo can only watch as she is hanged. Filled&nbsp;
with rage and grief, he hurls Frollo to his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:11.040
then vanishes. Years later, his skeleton is&nbsp;
found clutching hers in a mass pauper's grave.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.080
Not exactly the most obvious&nbsp;
choice for Disney - and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.080
changes they had to make to turn it into&nbsp;
a Disney story would prove controversial.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.240
Where better to learn how to&nbsp;
bring Paris to life onscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.200
than Paris itself? Early into production,&nbsp;
the core "Hunchback" team set up a studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.080
in the City of Lights to gather inspiration from&nbsp;
both the location, and from the author himself.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.080
"Victor Hugo lived just a few miles&nbsp;
from the studio - about 165 years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.480
Great name by the way: Victor Hugo! HAHA!"

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.360
They were even allowed unprecedented&nbsp;
access to Notre Dame. Kirk Wise can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.280
precisely recall the moment he discovered&nbsp;
the feeling he wanted the movie to conjure:

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.600
"Listening to this pipe organ in this&nbsp;
shadowy cathedral, with light filtering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.960
through the stained-glass windows [...]&nbsp;
I could feel it thudding in my chest."

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.240
A similar moment inspired lyricist Stephen&nbsp;
Schwartz to write "Out There." With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.640
composer Alan Menken's tune already in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.640
he sat in the bell tower early in the&nbsp;
morning, before the public was allowed in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.320
Schwartz looked out over Paris and felt himself&nbsp;
fully inhabit Quasimodo's world, saying,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.800
"By the time we left Paris, the song was written."

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.120
The filmmakers went through hellfire&nbsp;
to cast the voice of Quasimodo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.640
One unexpected candidate was legendary rocker&nbsp;
Meat Loaf, who auditioned for the directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.000
and composer Alan Menken. He was reportedly&nbsp;
great, but didn't fit their idea of the role.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
The most memorable audition, however,&nbsp;
came courtesy of Mandy Patinkin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.480
best known for "The Princess Bride" and&nbsp;
"Homeland." In what has since been dubbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.240
"The Patinkin Incident," the actor arrived at&nbsp;
his audition with his own accompanist. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.440
this accompanist had rearranged the audition song.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.160
These etiquette breaches didn't go down well&nbsp;
with Menken and lyricist Stephen Schwartz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:02.160
and when Patinkin took too long to get through&nbsp;
his audition, tempers flared into open argument.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.480
Disney ended up choosing Tom Hulce, who&nbsp;
was actually nervous about taking the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.400
But when the directors heard his audio for&nbsp;
the scene in which Quasimodo releases a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.560
bird he's been nursing, they knew he was&nbsp;
the guy they'd been looking for. The take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.560
was so heartfelt that it not only got him&nbsp;
the part, it's the one used in the movie.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.000
In the mid '90s, Demi Moore was one&nbsp;
of the most in-demand movie stars in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.320
Hollywood. Having found fame as a member&nbsp;
of the so-called "Brat Pack," Moore went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.560
on to star in hits including "Ghost," "A&nbsp;
Few Good Men," and "Indecent Proposal."

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.280
However, she wasn't Jeffrey Katzenberg's&nbsp;
number-one choice for the role of Esmeralda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.000
As supervising animator and storyboarder&nbsp;
Will Finn told Slash Film, the executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.440
wanted Cher for the role. Even Wise and Trousdale&nbsp;
didn't think of approaching Moore — but only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.680
because they didn't know they could. When&nbsp;
they told Katzenberg they were thinking of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.480
quote, "a Demi Moore-type person" for the role,&nbsp;
Katzenberg asked why they didn't just get Moore.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.320
Trousdale admitted,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.680
"We didn't know we were allowed to."

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.200
Reports about Moore being demanding turned out&nbsp;
to be false. As detail-oriented Disney fans will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.160
note, however, Esmeralda's singing voice was&nbsp;
provided by Broadway star Heidi Mollenhauer.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.000
Most Disney movies contain&nbsp;
an element of comic relief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.640
often in the form of silly sidekicks.&nbsp;
"Hunchback" leans on this trope too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:24.000
via Esmeralda's goat Djali, Phoebus's horse&nbsp;
Achilles, and Quasimodo's gargoyle friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.960
But introducing singing gargoyles into a story&nbsp;
this intense didn't go down well with everyone.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:34.400
The gargoyles' personalities changed dramatically&nbsp;
throughout production. Initially, they were going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.280
to be voiced by three of the era's most famous&nbsp;
late show hosts, Jay Leno, David Letterman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.360
and Arsenio Hall. Cyndi Lauper and Sam McMurray&nbsp;
were also nearly cast until Roy Disney himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.400
vetoed them. Ultimately, the gargoyles were played&nbsp;
by Charles Kimbrough, Mary Wickes, and Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.920
Alexander, with Jane Withers providing additional&nbsp;
dialogue after Wickes tragically passed away.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.800
Some viewers felt that goofy gargoyles don't&nbsp;
fit the movie's intense subject matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.160
As it turns out, some people&nbsp;
who worked on the movie agreed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.720
In Slash Film's oral history of "Hunchback,"&nbsp;
then-president of Disney Feature Animation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.920
Peter Schneider described their presence as,&nbsp;
quote, "icky," while writer Brenda Chapman said,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.160
"I wasn't sure if I liked how it ended up."

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.920
Though the entire movie pushes boundaries, there&nbsp;
are two songs in "Hunchback" audiences find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.760
especially shocking. The first is "Hellfire,"&nbsp;
in which Frollo struggles between bigoted piety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:32.400
and overpowering lust for Esmeralda.&nbsp;
The original storyboards even depicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.040
the vision of Esmeralda who dances in the&nbsp;
flames as naked. That idea was shot down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.400
but to everyone's surprise, the studio changed&nbsp;
nothing else. The demonic judges, the references&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:47.040
to "burning desire," the use of crucifixion&nbsp;
imagery — Disney was willing to back it all.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.280
The other eyebrow-raising song is&nbsp;
controversial for very different reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.680
"A Guy Like You," performed by Quasimodo and&nbsp;
the gargoyles, is a major tonal deviation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.720
from the rest of the movie. Lyricist Stephen&nbsp;
Schwartz was always unenthusiastic about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.800
but director Kirk Wise described&nbsp;
the song as a necessary interlude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:09.920
intended to keep the audience from, quote,&nbsp;
"sitting in this trough of despair for so long."

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:14.240
If you're a connoisseur of the Disney&nbsp;
Renaissance, then you've probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.320
loved a song by Alan Menken. He composed&nbsp;
the scores for "The Little Mermaid,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:22.320
"Beauty and the Beast," "Aladdin," and&nbsp;
"Pocahontas" before tackling "Hunchback."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.520
He went on to write the scores for&nbsp;
"Hercules" and "Tangled" as well.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.720
Menken won various Oscars for&nbsp;
the first four of those movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.800
including Best Original Musical Score for&nbsp;
"Pocahontas" and Best Original Song for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.680
"Colors of the Wind." He shares both trophies&nbsp;
with "Hunchback" lyricist Stephen Schwartz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.640
But out of all the music he's done, Menken is&nbsp;
most proud of the work he did on "Hunchback."

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.720
Menken told Slash Film

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.320
"It's the most ambitious score I've ever written."

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:52.720
Schwartz agreed that it's not just&nbsp;
Menken's best work, but his as well.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.520
"I think the music is just unbelievably good.&nbsp;
I think, as a lyricist, I was working at pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.400
much the top of my form. I have so many people&nbsp;
telling me it's their favorite Disney film."

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:07.920
When "Hunchback" came out in 1996, animation&nbsp;
had come a long way since Walt Disney's day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.760
But creating the crowds depicted&nbsp;
in the film's festival and mob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.000
sequences was still a major undertaking.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.120
Most of "Hunchback" was animated the old-fashioned&nbsp;
way: With carefully hand-drawn pictures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.720
painstakingly sequenced to&nbsp;
create the appearance of motion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:26.800
This is a lengthy enough process with&nbsp;
just one character, let alone hundreds.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:31.360
So for those crowd scenes, the CGI department&nbsp;
used then-cutting edge computer software to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.320
create several animated character templates.&nbsp;
Each was then repurposed dozens of times with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.360
different outfits, hairstyles, and accessories.&nbsp;
All of these models were then added into the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.000
and choreographed to respond to the main action.&nbsp;
The effect is a crowd that looks like it's made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:51.040
of individuals, created in a fraction of the&nbsp;
time a hand-drawn version would have taken.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.720
Many Disney villains are so over-the-top, they're&nbsp;
more fascinating — or funny — than frightening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:02.080
But there's nothing funny about Frollo –&nbsp;
his evil is deeply and recognizably human.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.840
"Ease up. Wait between lashes. Otherwise&nbsp;
the older lashes will dull him to the new."

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:14.160
The power he seeks isn't over an abstract group:&nbsp;
He specifically wants to control Esmeralda and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:20.000
destroy her community. He is so clearly evil&nbsp;
that Disney specifically changed his position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.280
from archdeacon, as it is in the novel,&nbsp;
to judge, so as not to insult the church.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:30.160
The scene in which Frollo threatens Esmeralda&nbsp;
inside the cathedral has a distinctly disturbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:34.640
tone — at least, if you're an adult. As the&nbsp;
"Hunchback" filmmakers recall, they thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.200
they were going to have a battle on their hands&nbsp;
trying to keep that especially sinister moment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.560
the movie. Surprisingly, they only had to make&nbsp;
one change. According to director Kirk Wise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.680
the ratings board was most troubled by&nbsp;
Frollo audibly sniffing Esmeralda's hair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.560
When the filmmakers agreed to lower&nbsp;
the volume of the sniff, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:56.720
given their "G" rating — a very important&nbsp;
thing for a Disney animated movie to have.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.520
Although "Hunchback" is a more serious&nbsp;
Disney movie, the animators working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:06.320
it still made room for in-jokes. There's a&nbsp;
reason some of the most brilliant Easter eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:11.040
of the last decade are in Disney movies,&nbsp;
after all: They're good at hiding them.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.640
"Hunchback" has multiple connections&nbsp;
to the studio's 1991 mega-hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:20.080
"Beauty and the Beast." Most obviously, it&nbsp;
reunited directors Kirk Wise and Gary Trousdale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.360
and composer Alan Menken and lyricist Stephen&nbsp;
Schwartz. In honor of their previous project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:29.600
a special guest makes an appearance&nbsp;
early on: Belle can be seen walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.880
through the market square during "Out There."&nbsp;
Naturally, she's reading as she strolls. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:39.440
Disney cameos can be glimpsed in that moment,&nbsp;
including the Magic Carpet from "Aladdin" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:44.480
Pumbaa from "The Lion King." Maybe don't point&nbsp;
that latter reference out to the kids, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:49.280
those guys walking there? They’ve got&nbsp;
Pumbaa on a spit… about to be roasted!

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.080
Another in-joke is hidden in plain sight, though&nbsp;
only the most hardcore animation nerds will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:58.720
notice it. Phoebus has the honor of being the&nbsp;
first modern Disney hero to have facial hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:02.560
a tongue-in-cheek poke at Jeffrey&nbsp;
Katzenberg. As Kirk Wise recalled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.240
Katzenberg was famously against&nbsp;
characters having facial hair. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:10.000
when he left the studio during production&nbsp;
on "Hunchback," the filmmakers jumped at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.040
the opportunity to express their newfound freedom&nbsp;
by giving their leading man a jaw-defining beard.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:20.400
"Hunchback" remains one of Disney's most ambitious&nbsp;
movies. Between the adventurous animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:25.680
searing score, distinctly morbid source material,&nbsp;
and attempted blend of comedy and drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:29.680
the film pushes the limits of people's&nbsp;
expectations for an animated movie.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.520
Although the studio had been supportive&nbsp;
of the film's dark tone during production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:37.040
the Disney marketing team presented&nbsp;
"Hunchback" as a colorful carnival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:43.200
entreating moviegoers to "Join the party!" And&nbsp;
merchandise tie-ins reinforced this upbeat theme.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:47.760
This bit of misdirection backfired, as it&nbsp;
set unrealistic expectations for confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:53.520
and unhappy viewers expecting an upbeat romp. And&nbsp;
the film's box office chances also took a hit when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:58.720
Jason Alexander said that though he loved the&nbsp;
film, he wouldn't want his young son to see it.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.400
Some critics appreciated the mature&nbsp;
themes and style "Hunchback" invokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:07.040
but ultimately few were satisfied. Those&nbsp;
expecting or wanting a pure Disney experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:11.840
found it too dark, while those looking for a&nbsp;
pure adaptation of Hugo's novel found it far too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:15.920
Disneyfied, with a thematically&nbsp;
jarring happy ending tacked on.

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.440
Still, though not a smash hit,&nbsp;
"Hunchback" did okay at the box office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:22.320
And some fans swear by it. Wise told Slash Film,

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:25.040
"I've had so many people come up to me and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.880
'This is my absolute favorite movie.' [...]&nbsp;
That makes all the difference in the world."

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:34.560
The relatively lackluster box office performance&nbsp;
of "Hunchback" didn't save it from the indignity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:39.200
of a direct-to-video sequel. "The Hunchback&nbsp;
of Notre Dame 2: The Secret of the Bell" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:43.600
released in 2002. Surprisingly, given&nbsp;
critical backlash to their silliness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:49.120
this sequel focuses heavily on the gargoyles,&nbsp;
who help Quasimodo save one of Notre Dame's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:55.040
illustrious bells. The bellringer also finally&nbsp;
finds love with circus performer Madellaine.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:13:00.480
And a successful 2014 stage musical has&nbsp;
stoked rumors that Disney will be making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.520
live action "Hunchback" remake in the style of&nbsp;
recent efforts like "Cinderella" and "Mulan."

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:10.800
Will moviegoers soon be watching Josh Gad&nbsp;
swing from cathedral spires? Considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:14.880
the animated version of "Hunchback" was&nbsp;
maybe Disney's most unlikely film ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.360
there's no telling what might happen next!

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.680
Check out one of our newest&nbsp;
videos right here! Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.640
even more Looper videos about your&nbsp;
favorite movies are coming soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:27.534
